|en|en|en"}]],"senses":[[49553,"neununddreißig","num","thirty-nine",0],[49554,"neununddreißig","noun","thirty-nine (number)",1,"f"]]},"wehrfähigste":{"entries":[["wehrfähigste",null,null,null]],"senses":[[581705,"wehrfähigste","adj","inflection of wehrfähig:",0],[581706,"wehrfähigste","adj","strong/mixed nominative/accusative feminine singular superlative degree",1],[581707,"wehrfähigste","adj","strong nominative/accusative plural superlative degree",2],[581708,"wehrfähigste","adj","weak nominative all-gender singular superlative degree",3],[581709,"wehrfähigste","adj","weak accusative feminine/neuter singular superlative degree",4]],"inflections":[["wehrfähigste","wehrfähig","feminine,nominative,singular,strong,superlative,without-article"]]},"zirkel":{"entries":[["zirkel",null,"/ˈt͡sɪrkəl/","Etymology tree\nProto-Indo-European *(s)ker-der.\nAncient Greek κίρκος (kírkos)der.\nAncient Greek κίρκινος (kírkinos)der.\nLatin circinusbor.\n▲\nLatin circulusinflu.?\nOld High German -ilinflu.?\nOld High German zirkil\nMiddle High German zirkel\nGerman Zirkel\nFrom Middle High German zirkel, from Old High German zirkil, from Latin circinus (“pair of compasses”), from Ancient Greek κίρκινος (kírkinos). The Old High German -il is due to either alteration after the Germanic tool suffix *-ilaz, or confluence with Latin circulus (“circle”); the -k- speaks in favour of the latter explanation. The sense “circle”, which appears in Middle High German, is at any rate borrowed from circulus. Both Latin words ultimately go back to Ancient Greek κίρκος (kírkos).",{"g":"m","decl":"|||s|||n|"}]],"senses":[[30836,"zirkel","noun","pair of compasses, compass (device to draw circles)",0,"m"],[30837,"zirkel","noun","circle (geometrical figure)",1,"m",["rare"]],[30838,"zirkel","noun","circle (group of persons with a common interest)",2,"m",["figuratively"]],[30839,"zirkel","noun","monogram of a student society.",3,"m"]],"related_forms":[[39176,"zirkel","zirkeln","derived"],[39177,"zirkel","zirk